03:19:33.520 --> 03:19:37.690
Welcome back, everybody. Welcome back, my naughty little things.

03:19:41.580 --> 03:19:45.620
My first guest tonight has had a brilliant film career, and she's still having it.

03:19:45.800 --> 03:19:50.040
She was in the movie Shine and in Gods and Monsters, in the classic Georgie Girl.

03:19:50.400 --> 03:19:53.030
She's in a new production of The Importance of Being Earnest,

03:19:53.390 --> 03:19:57.930
which is on now through March the 5th at the Amundsen Theatre right here in Los Angeles.

03:19:57.930 --> 03:20:00.000
Please welcome Lynn Redgrave, everybody.

03:20:16.160 --> 03:20:16.400
I'm Lynn.

03:20:16.640 --> 03:20:17.560
Oh, my God.

03:20:17.560 --> 03:20:20.300
Do I call you Lynn, or do you have a title?

03:20:20.520 --> 03:20:21.940
Are you Lady Redgrave?

03:20:22.180 --> 03:20:22.940
I'm Lynn.

03:20:23.210 --> 03:20:24.270
I'm Lynn, yes.

03:20:24.410 --> 03:20:25.350
I don't say your lady should.

03:20:25.370 --> 03:20:26.510
Cheeky monkey, I like.

03:20:26.630 --> 03:20:27.290
Oh, cheeky monkey.

03:20:27.290 --> 03:20:30.000
Because I call my grandchildren cheeky monkeys.

03:20:30.000 --> 03:20:31.420
and they think it's very funny.

03:20:31.600 --> 03:20:31.840
Yes.

03:20:31.840 --> 03:20:32.520
They love it.

03:20:32.680 --> 03:20:34.080
Yeah, I wasn't the person

03:20:34.080 --> 03:20:35.700
who originated the phrase cheeky monkey.

03:20:35.720 --> 03:20:36.540
No, you were not.

03:20:36.860 --> 03:20:38.120
I'm only too aware.

03:20:38.140 --> 03:20:40.460
Yeah, it's just tired old stuff.

03:20:40.720 --> 03:20:42.240
It's like stuff that's been around for years,

03:20:42.300 --> 03:20:43.360
like Shakespeare, really.

03:20:43.560 --> 03:20:44.000
Yes, yes, yes.

03:20:44.180 --> 03:20:45.640
Yes, just like Shakespeare.

03:20:46.570 --> 03:20:47.670
Yay, my lord.

03:20:47.790 --> 03:20:48.070
Yes.

03:20:48.070 --> 03:20:49.510
Yonder comes, yon cheeky monkey.

03:20:49.510 --> 03:20:50.110
That's right.

03:20:50.930 --> 03:20:53.190
Is this a cheeky monkey I see before me?

03:20:53.350 --> 03:20:53.590
Ah!

03:20:53.910 --> 03:20:55.550
Oh, better be careful.

03:20:55.690 --> 03:20:57.580
There are more cheeky monkeys in heaven and earth

03:20:57.580 --> 03:20:59.460
than I dreamt of in your philosophies, Horatio.

03:20:59.540 --> 03:21:00.000
Yeah.

03:21:00.350 --> 03:21:01.190
- Yes. - Indeed.

03:21:01.190 --> 03:21:02.010
- Yeah.

03:21:02.010 --> 03:21:02.850
- Yes.

03:21:02.850 --> 03:21:03.990
- So what is this play you're doing?

03:21:03.990 --> 03:21:05.070
The importance of being earnest?

03:21:05.070 --> 03:21:06.930
- I like it that you call it Ernest.

03:21:06.930 --> 03:21:07.990
- Ernest, yes.

03:21:07.990 --> 03:21:09.670
- We say Ernest, of course.

03:21:09.670 --> 03:21:10.770
- Well, that's because you're British.

03:21:10.770 --> 03:21:13.810
- I'm British and you're not.

03:21:13.810 --> 03:21:15.960
- Well, sort of I am.

03:21:15.960 --> 03:21:18.480
- You know, when I, you're from Glasgow.

03:21:18.480 --> 03:21:20.060
I played in Glasgow years ago.

03:21:20.060 --> 03:21:20.900
- I played there, too.

03:21:20.900 --> 03:21:23.080
- With a play called Billy Liar, I bet you did.

03:21:23.080 --> 03:21:24.300
You did different sort of playing.

03:21:24.300 --> 03:21:25.480
I was in a play.

03:21:25.480 --> 03:21:26.320
- You were in a play?

03:21:26.320 --> 03:21:27.720
- Yes.

03:21:27.720 --> 03:21:29.300
I was in a play called Billy Liar.

03:21:29.300 --> 03:21:30.000
- Billy Liar.

03:21:30.000 --> 03:21:33.720
You know in England how people say, oh, bloody hell, you know, bloody.

03:21:34.000 --> 03:21:35.060
Bloody, yes, they say bloody.

03:21:35.100 --> 03:21:37.520
And there was still the Lord Chamberlain in those days when I did.

03:21:37.540 --> 03:21:38.400
This was back in the 60s.

03:21:38.420 --> 03:21:39.060
Lord Chamberlain.

03:21:39.260 --> 03:21:43.180
The Lord Chamberlain was the man who made sure that you didn't do anything obscene on the stage.

03:21:43.200 --> 03:21:45.320
It was the British censor, that's right.

03:21:45.320 --> 03:21:47.440
They would stop people cursing on stage.

03:21:47.440 --> 03:21:48.240
So there were all these...

03:21:48.240 --> 03:21:49.640
We have something similar at CBS.

03:21:51.320 --> 03:21:53.220
I bet he's a cheeky monkey, too.

03:21:53.220 --> 03:21:54.340
He is a cheeky monkey, yeah.

03:21:55.560 --> 03:22:00.000
But so the father in the play had to say, oh, bloody hell.

03:22:00.000 --> 03:22:01.880
Billy, you know, bloody, bloody, bloody.

03:22:01.880 --> 03:22:04.060
And the cops came in one day

03:22:04.060 --> 03:22:06.060
and they counted the bloodies. This was in Glasgow

03:22:06.060 --> 03:22:08.040
because you're a very uptight place sometimes.

03:22:08.080 --> 03:22:08.780
Oh, yes, yes.

03:22:09.220 --> 03:22:12.180
And they counted the bloodies and they gave us a citation

03:22:12.180 --> 03:22:13.540
for having too many bloodies.

03:22:13.640 --> 03:22:15.900
You got a ticket? Well, not me personally

03:22:15.900 --> 03:22:17.260
but the show did.

03:22:17.520 --> 03:22:20.550
Really? Yeah. Too many bloodies.

03:22:21.290 --> 03:22:22.210
Who paid?

03:22:22.370 --> 03:22:23.870
Who paid the ticket? Did you pay it?

03:22:23.870 --> 03:22:25.070
No, I didn't have any money.

03:22:26.250 --> 03:22:27.840
I didn't notice the people of Glasgow

03:22:27.840 --> 03:22:29.860
being particularly uptight. Mind you, I was drunk.

03:22:30.000 --> 03:22:32.300
Yeah, possibly.

03:22:32.300 --> 03:22:34.300
Yeah, so that's it.

03:22:34.300 --> 03:22:36.300
Drunk or a child? Possibly.

03:22:36.300 --> 03:22:38.300
You could have been a child in the early 60s.

03:22:38.300 --> 03:22:40.830
I could have been a drunken child.

03:22:40.830 --> 03:22:42.830
What about, now your family are very famous.

03:22:42.830 --> 03:22:44.830
The Redgraves are, they've acted for years.

03:22:44.830 --> 03:22:46.830
You've acted with one, with you.

03:22:46.830 --> 03:22:48.830
I have my niece Gemma Redgraves.

03:22:48.830 --> 03:22:50.830
Well, she acted and I kind of like ran around going,

03:22:50.830 --> 03:22:52.810
"Oh, cheeky monkey!" and everything.

03:22:52.810 --> 03:22:54.810
She's very good. She's wonderful, yeah.

03:22:54.810 --> 03:22:56.820
Is that why you went into acting? Because of your family?

03:22:56.820 --> 03:23:00.000
No, well, it's hard to say that it isn't.

03:23:00.180 --> 03:23:00.940
There's so many of us.

03:23:01.060 --> 03:23:02.820
But I didn't think I was going to act.

03:23:02.880 --> 03:23:05.020
My sister Vanessa was always going to be an actor.

03:23:05.120 --> 03:23:07.070
My brother Corin, father of Gemma.

03:23:07.070 --> 03:23:08.610
Father of Gemma, Redgrave, yes.

03:23:08.690 --> 03:23:10.090
And my parents, my grandparents.

03:23:10.510 --> 03:23:16.260
And one day in 1958, I went to a production of Shakespeare's Twelfth Night.

03:23:16.640 --> 03:23:18.760
Yay, Cheeky Monkey, there in Illyria.

03:23:20.600 --> 03:23:22.370
This is Illyria Lady, that's right.

03:23:22.510 --> 03:23:23.790
This is Illyria Lady, Cheeky.

03:23:23.950 --> 03:23:24.350
Cheeky.

03:23:24.590 --> 03:23:29.270
And it was directed by Peter Hall, who is directing The Importance of Being Ernest.

03:23:29.550 --> 03:23:30.000
Ah, yeah.

03:23:30.410 --> 03:23:33.570
I fell in love with the idea of

03:23:33.570 --> 03:23:35.310
disappearing inside these

03:23:35.310 --> 03:23:37.710
characters. Shakespearean characters?

03:23:37.710 --> 03:23:39.790
Well, not just Shakespeare, no. Just to turn

03:23:39.790 --> 03:23:41.830
into other people. I thought being me was really dull.

03:23:41.930 --> 03:23:43.870
Oh, you're lovely. Why don't you be yourself in a

03:23:43.870 --> 03:23:45.970
play? You know, now, I do from time

03:23:45.970 --> 03:23:47.850
to time. Hamlet? Lynn Redgrave?

03:23:47.930 --> 03:23:49.830
Yes. Lynn Redgrave? Hamlet, you can just meet him.

03:23:49.970 --> 03:23:50.910
There you are. Yeah, yeah.

03:23:51.570 --> 03:23:53.350
Could you do that? No, I'd like, what, be Hamlet?

03:23:53.450 --> 03:23:54.990
No, just mess around with a play.

03:23:55.660 --> 03:23:57.680
I do. I write plays. Do you really?

03:23:57.780 --> 03:24:00.000
Yes. I didn't know you wrote plays. I know, you don't have to know.

03:24:00.120 --> 03:24:10.230
everything about me. I feel it's my job too. I feel that's very fresh of you. But I'm liking

03:24:10.230 --> 03:24:14.890
it. We'll have to take a break. We'll be right back with Lynn Redgrave, everybody.

